# Paths and fragments that name nothing real BY DESIGN.
#
# One per line. A `docs/...` path here is exempt from check_source_doc_refs.py;
# a `path#fragment` entry is exempt from check_doc_links.py's anchor half.
#
# Legitimate entries: help-text examples, output destinations a run creates,
# test fixtures, template placeholders, in-app routes that look like anchors.
#
# NOT legitimate: a reference that broke when a plan was filed. Repoint it —
# the reference is prose that records why the code is shaped that way.

# ── Shipped by the scaffold itself ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The tree arrives carrying these, and every one is in the legitimate list
# above. Without them `check docs` — and the CI gate that runs the same
# checker — is red on a tree nobody has touched yet.
#
# The checker's own docstring example, and two fixture names a unit test
# creates in a temporary tree:
docs/TODO/x.md
docs/a.md
docs/b.md
#
# An output destination the Saturday report job creates, named at `agentic`
# and above. Harmless at `core`, where nothing references it.
docs/reports/regular/repository-report.md

# ── sky.boss: highlighter fixtures ──────────────────────────────────────────
# `skyboss/highlight.py` tints a followed line by SHAPE — a timestamp, a number,
# a path — so its sample lines and its tests must contain path-shaped text to
# prove a path is tinted at all. These name nothing and are not meant to: they
# are the input to a highlighter, not a reference to a document.
#
# Replacing them with real paths would be worse than the exemption. The fixture
# would then depend on a document continuing to exist for a reason unrelated to
# what it is testing, and deleting that document would break the highlighter's
# tests with a message about tinting.
docs/plan.md
docs/AGENTIC_AUTOMATION.md
